    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:authorConclusion",
      "object": "Factors that have a significant relationship with disordered eating are performance perfectionism and weight management. A desire to control weight is associated with disordered eating behaviors in male high school cross country runners as determined from the modest correlation coefficient for the weight management subscale and the EAT-26. There was no significant correlation for the physical appearance subscale of the EMI-2 and the EAT-26. No significant relationships were found between disordered eating behaviors and social pressure on body shape, social pressure on eating and athlete identity."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:limites",
      "object": "The results are generalizable only to school districts of similar size and demographic makeup. The questionnaire were completed on one occasion by runners during the sumer prior to the start of the season. The study was limited by a low number of participants relative to the number of runners on the teams and number of high schools in the school district."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:perspectives",
      "object": "The present study did not investigate the reasons for dissatisfaction with current weight status, future studies may seek more information relative to the reasons. For example, do male cross country runners feels a need to be more muscular ? Do the bodies of male cross country runners not fit the ideal ?"
    },
